---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFileBase64 from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeFileBase64.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="svg-to-base64">
  <DevEncodeFileBase64 client:load mode="svg" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>SVG to Base64 or URL-encoding?</h2>
    <p>
      For SVG, Base64 is usually the wrong choice. SVG is text, so it can go into a <code>data:</code> URI
      directly, with only the few characters that break CSS escaped. The result is shorter than Base64, the
      markup stays readable in the stylesheet, and it compresses better in transfer.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Base64</th><th>URL-encoded</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Typical size versus source</td><td>+33%</td><td>+5% to +15%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Readable in the stylesheet</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gzip / Brotli friendly</td><td>Poor: byte alignment is lost</td><td>Good</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Safe in an <code>&lt;img src&gt;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tolerant of hand-editing</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, with care</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use the toggle above to switch between the two and compare the character counts for your file. Base64
      is better in one case: a build pipeline or CMS that damages percent signs or quotes in stylesheet
      values.
    </p>

    <h2>What must be escaped</h2>
    <p>
      In a URL-encoded SVG data URI, you must handle <code>%</code>, <code>#</code>, <code>&lt;</code>,
      <code>&gt;</code> and the quote characters. This tool uses the method that works in every CSS parser:
      it replaces the double quotes inside the markup with single quotes, wraps the whole URI in double
      quotes, and percent-encodes the rest. A raw <code>#</code> is the usual failure: it starts a fragment
      identifier and cuts off the icon without an error.
    </p>

    <h2>You cannot recolour a data URI</h2>
    <p>
      Inside <code>background-image</code>, an SVG is an image. <code>fill: currentColor</code>, CSS custom
      properties and <code>:hover</code> rules in your page no longer reach it. There are three workarounds,
      best first:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Inline the <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> element in your HTML or component. The icon then inherits
        <code>currentColor</code> and all other CSS rules.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use <code>mask-image</code> in place of <code>background-image</code>, with a solid
        <code>background-color</code> behind it. The mask gives the shape and CSS gives the colour.
      </li>
      <li>
        Generate one data URI per colour at build time. This is crude but works for two or three states.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Sanitise before you inline</h2>
    <p>
      An SVG is executable markup: it can contain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, event handlers and external
      references. A data URI in <code>background-image</code> renders in a restricted mode where scripts do
      not run, but the same file inlined as an <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> element in your DOM can run them. If
      the file came from a user, an icon marketplace or a design handoff, read it first. It is text, so you
      can.
    </p>

    <h2>Trim the markup first</h2>
    <p>
      Editor exports are verbose. Before you encode, remove the editor metadata, any <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>
      and <code>&lt;desc&gt;</code> you do not need, empty groups, and a <code>width</code>/<code>height</code>
      pair that conflicts with your CSS. Keep the <code>viewBox</code>, which makes the icon scale. The SVG to
      Base64 converter collapses runs of whitespace, which is always safe. It does not rewrite path data,
      because rounded coordinates change rendering in ways a preview may not show.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
